{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgg8uZrCJ7yxRyiPwtPSW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't give reliable values for Dq, B and C from these four bands. The three quartet bands alone are already inconsistent within the model.\n\n**Quartet fit (Dq and B only).** The three spin-allowed bands depend only on Dq and B.\n\n- Take the ground term ⁴T₁(F) as the lower root E₁ of the ⁴T₁(F)–⁴T₁(P) matrix, measured from the free-ion ⁴F energy. The matrix is [[−6Dq, 6B], [6B, 15B + 6Dq]].\n- The other terms lie at ⁴T₂ = 2Dq and ⁴A₂ = 12Dq on the same scale.\n- Then ν(⁴A₂) − ν(⁴T₂) = 10Dq = 15 200 − 7 150 = 8 050 cm⁻¹, so Dq = 805 cm⁻¹.\n- The ⁴T₂ band gives 2Dq − E₁ = 7 150, so E₁ = −5 540 cm⁻¹.\n- Requiring E₁ to be a root of the matrix gives 36B² − 10 650B − 7 362 700 = 0, so B ≈ 624 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Prediction for the third band.** With these values, the upper root of the matrix puts ⁴T₁(P) at about 20 400 cm⁻¹ above the ground state. The observed band is at 19 200 cm⁻¹, a misfit of about 1 200 cm⁻¹. The three spin-allowed bands therefore cannot be fitted by a single (Dq, B) pair to anything like experimental accuracy.\n\n**Why C is not determined.** C comes only from the spin-forbidden ²T₁(2) band at 21 130 cm⁻¹. Its energy comes from a doublet matrix that mixes several ²T₁ terms and depends on Dq, B and C together. The quartet inconsistency means the Dq and B fed into that matrix are not trustworthy. The result for C would then be set by the error in the quartet fit, not by the doublet physics.\n\n**Likely causes of the misfit.** The three band positions can be shifted or mixed by effects outside the simple Racah–ligand-field model. These include spin–orbit coupling in the ⁴T₁ ground term, Jahn–Teller or low-symmetry splitting, and doublet–quartet mixing. The ⁴A₂ band is also a weak two-electron transition, so its position is uncertain.\n\nI am therefore not reporting numbers. The only firm result is